items/thm-tdmath-spatial-mean-information.md:89:4.1 Since $Z\in L^1(P)$, dominated convergence gives $\|Z-Z_M\|_1\to0$. Cube averages are $L^1$ contractions, so the averages of $Z$ are Cauchy in $L^1$: for large $M$ their distance from the convergent averages of $Z_M$ is at most $\|Z-Z_M\|_1$. Let their $L^1$ limit be $s$. Every cube average has mean $E_PZ=\gamma$, hence $E_Ps=\gamma$. For each coordinate shift $e_j$, the averaging index cube and its translate have symmetric difference of exactly $2N^{d-1}$ indices, so $\|U_j A_NZ-A_NZ\|_1\leq2\|Z\|_1/N$. Passing to the $L^1$ limit gives $U_js=s$. These generators imply invariance under every integer translation. [A1, F2, F5, step 3.1, construct]
items/thm-csm-dk-compact-iid-propagation-of-chaos.md:136:4.1 The joint law of the finite particle vector is permutation invariant by [F4] and iid symmetry [F1]. Given the finite-particle configuration at time $s$, draw $k$ indices independently and uniformly from $\{1,\ldots,N\}$. The resulting tuple has conditional law $\mu_N(s)^{\otimes k}$. The chance of an index collision is at most $\binom{k}{2}/N$ by a union bound. After averaging over the exchangeable particle vector, conditioning the index sampling on no collision makes the tuple's law equal to that of the first $k$ particles. Thus the expectation of $\Psi$ under the true $k$-law and under $\mu_N(s)^{\otimes k}$ differs by at most $2\|\Psi\|_\infty\binom{k}{2}/N$. For any coupling $\pi$ of $\mu_N(s)$ and $\mu_s$, its $k$-fold product from [F6] couples their product laws, and the sum-metric Lipschitz bound gives a test-integral difference at most $k\operatorname{Lip}(\Psi)\int d_Z\,d\pi$. Taking the infimum over $\pi$, then expectation, and using [F5] gives the second term in the displayed bound. The estimate tends to zero uniformly for $|s|\le T$, establishing fixed-$k$ propagation of chaos in the stated test class. [F1, F4, F5, F6, step 3.1, algebra] ∎
