items/pthm-em-causal-dispersive-response.md:112:2.1 For the instantaneous part write $\chi_\infty=S+A$ with $S$ symmetric and $A$ antisymmetric, as in the Statement. For any real $C^1$ history, the product rule of [L4] gives $\frac{d}{dt}(E\cdot SE)=2\,E\cdot S\partial_tE$, while $E\cdot AE=0$ because $(E\cdot AE)^{\mathsf T}=E\cdot A^{\mathsf T}E=-E\cdot AE$; hence $E\cdot\partial_t(\varepsilon_0\chi_\infty E)=\frac{d}{dt}(\frac{\varepsilon_0}{2}E\cdot SE)+\varepsilon_0E\cdot A\partial_tE$, the exact split displayed. For a periodic history the storage derivative has zero period average, and with $E=\operatorname{Re}(e e^{-i\omega t})$ the remaining average is $\frac{\varepsilon_0\omega}{2}\operatorname{Im}(e^*Ae)$; since $\chi_\infty$ is real, $\chi_\infty^*=\chi_\infty^{\mathsf T}$ and $(\chi_\infty-\chi_\infty^*)/(2i)=A/i$, so $M_{\mathrm{tot}}=M_K+A/i$. In the explicit example $\chi_\infty=A$ has $\chi_\infty e=(i,-1,0)^{\mathsf T}$ and $e^*\chi_\infty e=2i$ for $e=(1,i,0)^{\mathsf T}$; directly $E=(\cos\omega t,\sin\omega t,0)$ and $\chi_\infty\partial_tE=\omega E$, so the work is $\varepsilon_0\omega$ at every time, while $e=(1,-i,0)^{\mathsf T}$ gives $E=(\cos\omega t,-\sin\omega t,0)$, $\chi_\infty\partial_tE=-\omega E$ and averaged work $-\varepsilon_0\omega$. More generally a real antisymmetric $A$ with $A_{jk}=a\ne0$ gives $e^*Ae=2ia\ne0$ for $e=e_j+ie_k$, and the conjugate amplitude gives $-2ia$. Consequently the instantaneous response alone, when $A\ne0$, fails all-amplitude passivity; the full response must still be tested using $M_K+A/i$. A nonzero cycle work also precludes purely quadratic storage for every history, so that property requires $A=0$. [step 1.4, L4, L6]
items/ex-em-dielectric-interface-example.md:85:1.1 Localize a test by a smooth partition of unity [L3] to finitely many rectangular boxes compactly contained in $U$; for boxes meeting the plane choose the height interval to straddle $z=0$. It suffices to prove the identity for each localized test and sum. Let $F\in\{E,B,D,H\}$ and let $\psi$ be supported in one such box. For fixed $(x,y,t)$ with $z\ne0$ the map $z\mapsto F(x,y,z,t)$ is $C^1$ on each part of that height interval and has the stated one-sided $C^1$ extensions at zero; applying [L1] on the two closed height subintervals and summing gives $\int_{\mathbb R}F\,\partial_z\psi\,dz=-[F](x,y,0,t)\,\psi(x,y,0,t)-\int_{\mathbb R}\{\partial_zF\}\psi\,dz$, because the outer boundary terms vanish by compact support; integrating over $x,y,t$ by [L2], with the plane $\{z=0\}$ null by [L4] so that the split into the two half-spaces is exact, gives $\langle\partial_zF,\psi\rangle=\int_U\{\partial_zF\}\psi+\langle[F]\delta_S,\psi\rangle$ componentwise, i.e. $\operatorname{div}F=\{\operatorname{div}F\}+e_z\cdot[F]\,\delta_S$ and $\operatorname{curl}F=\{\operatorname{curl}F\}+e_z\times[F]\,\delta_S$ by [L6], the tangential derivatives contributing no sheet term. [L1, L2, L3, L4, L6]
