items/lem-qft-fock-ccr-car.md:129:2.2 For $n\ge1$ and a symmetric elementary tensor $v_1\vee\cdots\vee v_n:=P_{s,n}(v_1\otimes\cdots\otimes v_n)$, contraction and creation give $a(h)(v_1\vee\cdots\vee v_n)=n^{-1/2}\sum_{j=1}^n\langle h,v_j\rangle_2 \,v_1\vee\cdots\widehat{v_j}\cdots\vee v_n$ and $a^\dagger(k)(v_1\vee\cdots\vee v_n)=\sqrt{n+1}\, P_{s,n+1}(k\otimes v_1\otimes\cdots\otimes v_n)$, by expanding the permutation averages [F2]. In the composition $a(h)a^\dagger(k)$, the contraction term for the newly inserted $k$ is $\langle h,k\rangle_2(v_1\vee\cdots\vee v_n)$; each of the other $n$ contraction terms is exactly the corresponding term of $a^\dagger(k)a(h)$. Hence their difference is $\langle h,k\rangle_2I$. On sector zero, $a(h)\Omega=0$, $a^\dagger(k)\Omega=k$, and $a_1(h)k=\langle h,k\rangle_2$ give the same commutator directly. Symmetry of $P_{s,n}$ makes two creations commute; two contractions commute because contracting distinct tensor slots in either order gives the same sum. Finite elementary symmetric tensors are dense in each sector and the sector maps are bounded there, so the identities hold on all of $\mathcal D_{\rm fin}$. [F1, F2, step 1.2, algebra]
items/cor-tgr-killing-no-work-support.md:45:Use the time-oriented Lorentzian open set, smooth metric, metric-selected connection, Killing field, positively oriented finite coordinate slab with compact closure, and finite-slab conventions of [[lem-tgr-killing-energy-normalization]], including its definitions of $h_{ij},n_a,w,j_\xi,H_\xi$ and the net outward side flux $\Phi_{j_\xi}(B)$. Assume $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$ as required by its connection construction. Let $T^{ab}$ be smooth and symmetric and let $f^b$ be a smooth vector field such that $\nabla_aT^{ab}=f^b$; this replaces the conserved-stress hypothesis for this corollary. If $f^b\xi_b=0$, then $\nabla_a j_\xi^a=0$, and, conditional on the finite-slab balance theorem [[thm-tgr-finite-current-production-balance]] at its actual interface, the finite slice charges satisfy $H_\xi(x_0^+)-H_\xi(x_0^-)+\Phi_{j_\xi}(B)=0$. Thus the endpoint charges are equal exactly when the net side flux is zero. In the static subcase, assume $\xi=\mathcal N_\xi u$ with $\mathcal N_\xi>0$; the algebraic zero-work condition $u_bf^b=0$ then implies $f^b\xi_b=0$. No infinite-domain or exhaustion limit is asserted.
items/lem-qft-dirac-covariance-spectrum.md:110:3.1 By steps 2.1 and 2.2 the particle and conjugate antiparticle test maps extend to unitaries on $\mathcal H_+$ and $\overline{\mathcal H_-}$. Their direct sum $T(\tilde g)$ is unitary. Define $U(\tilde g)=\bigoplus_{n\ge0}T(\tilde g)^{\otimes_a n}$ on the antisymmetric Fock sectors. Each tensor power is unitary by its isometric action on elementary tensors and inverse tensor power; it commutes with the signed permutation average and therefore restricts to a unitary on the antisymmetric sector. The direct sum is unitary and fixes $\Omega$. On elementary wedge tensors the insertion/contraction formulas of [F5] give $U c^\dagger(h)U^{-1}=c^\dagger(Th)$ and $U c(h)U^{-1}=c(Th)$; density extends these identities to the finite-particle domain. Thus $U(\tilde g)\Psi(F)U(\tilde g)^{-1}=\Psi(\tau_{\tilde g}F)$. Direct substitution in the affine and spinor formulas gives $\tau_{\tilde g_2}\tau_{\tilde g_1}=\tau_{\tilde g_2\tilde g_1}$: the coordinate argument is $(\Lambda_2\Lambda_1)^{-1}(x-a_2-\Lambda_2a_1)$ and the matrix factor is $(S_2S_1)^{-\dagger}=S_2^{-\dagger}S_1^{-\dagger}$. By [F8] the Lorentz factors compose within $SO^+(1,3)$; by the definition of $\widetilde G$ its triples are closed under this product. Hence $T$ and its Fock lift $U$ obey the same product law and $U$ is a representation. [A1, F1, F4, F5, F8, step 2.1, step 2.2, algebra]
items/lem-gr-universal-development.md:151:[L10] Once $Q$ is second countable, its Hausdorff locally Euclidean smooth atlas makes it a smooth manifold, and Countable Choice gives a smooth partition of unity subordinate to its member cover. A finite positive weighted sum of vectors in one future timelike cone remains future timelike ([[def-smooth-manifold]], [[thm-smooth-partitions-of-unity-exist-on-manifolds]], [[lem-timelike-cone-has-two-convex-components]]).
items/lem-gr-universal-development.md:173:7.1 (A smooth global orientation representative.) The quotient is now a smooth manifold in the full sense of [L10]. Choose a subordinate locally finite smooth partition of unity $(\rho_\alpha)$ for the member cover and put $\bar N=\sum_\alpha\rho_\alpha(\pi|_{U_\alpha})_*N_\alpha$, extending each summand by zero outside $Q_\alpha$. Each summand is smooth because its support lies in $Q_\alpha$; the sum is locally finite. At every point the nonzero summands are positive multiples of vectors in the same future timelike component by step 4.1 and at least one coefficient is positive since the coefficients sum to one. Cone convexity makes $\bar N$ smooth, nowhere zero and future timelike. Every member map therefore preserves time orientation in the sense of preserving future cones. The metric and matter fields are the descended fields of step 4.1; the particular representative $\bar N$ is not claimed unique. [step 4.1, step 6.1, L10, construct]
items/lem-tgr-timelike-scalar-acceleration.md:199:4.1 For compact real tests $f,g$, the linear change $v=(\tau+\tau')/2$, $s=\tau-\tau'$ has Jacobian of absolute value one and takes $f(\tau)g(\tau')$ to a compact smooth test $\varphi(v,s)$. On the compact average-time projection of its support, write $A(v)=2\ell e^{-\kappa v}>0$ and $B(v)=2\ell e^{\kappa v}>0$. The factors $Y-X+i\epsilon$ and $Y+X-i\epsilon$ are respectively $-A(v)(x-i\epsilon/A(v))$ and $B(v)(x-i\epsilon/B(v))$, where $x=\sinh(\kappa s/2)$. For positive $\alpha,\beta$ the identity $$\frac{1}{(x-i\alpha\epsilon)(x-i\beta\epsilon)} =\int_0^1\frac{dt}{(x-i((1-t)\alpha+t\beta)\epsilon)^2}$$ follows by integrating the derivative of $[x-i((1-t)\alpha+t\beta)\epsilon]^{-1}$ in $t$ and dividing by $i(\beta-\alpha)\epsilon$ when $\alpha\ne\beta$; for $\alpha=\beta$ both sides agree directly. It shows that both dampings have the same boundary. To justify that boundary, for a compact smooth test $\psi$ choose an even compact smooth $\chi$ equal to one near zero and write $\psi(x)=\psi(0)\chi(x)+x\rho(x)$, where $\rho$ is smooth and compactly supported. The constant term satisfies $$\int\frac{\chi(x)}{x-i\lambda\epsilon}\,dx =i\lambda\epsilon\int\frac{\chi(x)}{x^2+(\lambda\epsilon)^2}\,dx\longrightarrow i\pi$$ for each $\lambda>0$, since its odd real part integrates to zero. The other term is $x\rho(x)/(x-i\lambda\epsilon)=\rho(x)+ i\lambda\epsilon\rho(x)/(x-i\lambda\epsilon)$; the last integral is $O(\epsilon|\log\epsilon|)$ uniformly for $\lambda$ in a compact positive interval. Let $\delta_0$ denote evaluation at zero, $\langle\delta_0,\psi\rangle=\psi(0)$, and define the distributional derivative by $\langle T',\psi\rangle=-\langle T,\psi'\rangle$ for compact smooth tests $\psi$, so $\langle\delta_0',\psi\rangle=-\psi'(0)$. Thus $(x-i0)^{-1}=\operatorname{PV}(1/x)+i\pi\delta_0$, where $\operatorname{PV}(1/x)$ acts by $\lim_{\delta\downarrow0}\int_{|x|>\delta}\psi(x)/x\,dx$. Differentiating this distributional identity gives $(x-i0)^{-2}=\operatorname{Pf}(1/x^2)-i\pi\delta_0'$ and $$\left\langle\operatorname{Pf}(1/x^2),\psi\right\rangle =\lim_{\delta\downarrow0}\left(\int_{|x|>\delta}\frac{\psi(x)}{x^2}\,dx-\frac{2\psi(0)}{\delta}\right).$$ Integration by parts on $|x|>\delta$ identifies this finite part with $-(\operatorname{PV}(1/x))'$. The bounds are uniform when the positive damping coefficient ranges in a compact subinterval of $(0,\infty)$, as it does for $v$ in a compact set. To identify the pullback with the usual $s-i0$ boundary, note that $\sinh(bs-i\epsilon b)=\sinh(bs)-i\epsilon b\cosh(bs)+O(\epsilon^2)$. More generally, for a positive smooth regulator $\lambda(v,x)$, Taylor expand $\lambda=\lambda_0(v)+\lambda_1(v)x+x^2r(v,x)$. The linear part factors as $$x-i\epsilon(\lambda_0+\lambda_1x) =(1-i\epsilon\lambda_1)\left(x-\frac{i\epsilon\lambda_0}{1-i\epsilon\lambda_1}\right).$$ Its pole has positive imaginary part comparable to $\epsilon$ and real displacement $O(\epsilon^2)$, so the preceding test-subtraction calculation gives the same boundary. The quadratic remainder changes the inverse square by at most $C\epsilon x^2/(x^2+\epsilon^2)^{3/2}$ on a small interval; its integral is $O(\epsilon|\log\epsilon|)$. Thus the smooth positive regulator, including the one induced by $\sinh(b(s-i\epsilon))$, has the same boundary as the constant positive regulator. The change $x=\sinh(\kappa s/2)$ is orientation-preserving with positive derivative. Consequently the test-paired limit of step 3.2 is $$C_a(s)=-\frac{1}{16\pi^2\ell^2}\operatorname{csch}^2\!\left(\frac{\kappa(s-i0)}{2}\right) =-\frac{a^2}{16\pi^2c^4}\operatorname{csch}^2\!\left(\frac{a(s-i0)}{2c}\right).$$ Thus $\langle K_{\gamma_a}f,K_{\gamma_a}g\rangle_2$ equals $\left\langle C_a(s),\int f(t)g(t-s)\,dt\right\rangle$. This proves stationarity and the stated covariance boundary from ordinary regulated shell pairings. To verify temperedness explicitly, set $q=\lim_{\epsilon\downarrow0}\coth(\kappa(s-i\epsilon)/2)$ in the distribution sense and $r(s)=\coth(\kappa s/2)-2/(\kappa s)$ for $s\ne0$. The Taylor expansion of $\coth$ at zero extends $r$ smoothly there, and $r'(s)=-(\kappa/2)\operatorname{csch}^2(\kappa s/2)+2/(\kappa s^2)$ is bounded: its apparent singularity cancels at zero, and both terms are bounded at infinity. Since $1/(s-i0)=\operatorname{PV}(1/s)+i\pi\delta_0$, the decomposition is $q=(2/\kappa)\operatorname{PV}(1/s)+r+(2\pi i/\kappa)\delta_0$. By the derivative definition and $q_\epsilon\to q$ on compact smooth tests, $q_\epsilon'\to q'$. Direct differentiation of $q_\epsilon(s)=\coth(\kappa(s-i\epsilon)/2)$ then gives $C_a=(a^2/(8\pi^2c^4\kappa))q'$. For $m,n\ge0$ set $p_{m,n}(\varphi)=\sup_s(1+s^2)^{m/2}|\varphi^{(n)}(s)|$, a Schwartz seminorm. For $\varphi\in\mathcal S(\mathbb R)$, the symmetric principal-value formula gives $\left|\left\langle\operatorname{PV}(1/s),\varphi'\right\rangle\right|\le 2p_{0,2}(\varphi)+C p_{2,1}(\varphi),$ because subtracting $\varphi'(0)$ on $|s|<1$ bounds the local integral by $2\sup|\varphi''|$, while the $|s|\ge1$ integral is bounded by $p_{2,1}\int_{|s|\ge1}[|s|(1+s^2)]^{-1}ds$. Also $|\int r'(s)\varphi(s)ds|\le C p_{2,0}(\varphi)$ and $|\langle\delta_0',\varphi\rangle|\le p_{0,1}(\varphi)$. Hence $|\langle C_a,\varphi\rangle|\le C_{a,c}\bigl(p_{0,2}(\varphi)+p_{2,1}(\varphi)+p_{2,0}(\varphi)+p_{0,1}(\varphi)\bigr),$ a finite Schwartz-seminorm bound. By [F2], $C_a\in\mathcal S'(\mathbb R)$, as required for its subsequent Fourier transform. [F1, F2, step 3.2, step 1.2, algebra]
items/thm-qft-regular-formal-causal-factorization.md:95:[F7] Every open cover admits a locally finite smooth partition of unity with compact supports subordinate to cover members ([[lem-test-function-cutoffs-and-euclidean-localization]]).
items/thm-qft-regular-formal-causal-factorization.md:105:2.1 Put $C=A-B$. Every $z=x-y\in C$ either has $z^0>0$ or is spacelike: if $z^0\le0$ and it is not spacelike, then $z\in C_-$, excluded by $A\succcurlyeq_{\mathrm{out}}B$; this also excludes the zero difference. Thus $C$ lies in the open set $\Omega=U_+\cup U_{\mathrm{sp}}$, where $U_+=\{z:z^0>0\}$ and $U_{\mathrm{sp}}=\{z:(z^0)^2-|\mathbf z|^2<0\}$. By [F7], the cover $\{U_+,U_{\mathrm{sp}}\}$ of $\Omega$ has a locally finite smooth partition of unity subordinate to these sets. Any compactly supported relative test in $\Omega$ meets only finitely many partition functions. On each $U_+$ piece [F1] gives $H_\mu=W_\mu$; on each $U_{\mathrm{sp}}$ piece [F1] gives agreement with both Wightman orderings, which coincide there by step 1.1. Therefore $\widetilde H_\mu-\widetilde W_\mu$ vanishes on $\Omega$. The open set $O=\{(x,y):x-y\in\Omega\}$ contains $A\times B$, and the lift [F4] sends every test supported in $O$ to a relative test supported in $\Omega$. Hence $\widetilde H_\mu-\widetilde W_\mu$ vanishes on an open neighborhood of $A\times B$. [F1, F2, F4, F7, step 1.1, construct]
items/thm-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-transverse-continuum-free-fock-hamiltonian.md:154:The elementary tensor span is dense, and permutation unitaries act on tensor factors by $U_\pi(f_1\otimes\cdots\otimes f_n)=f_{\widehat{\pi}^{-1}(1)}\otimes\cdots\otimes f_{\widehat{\pi}^{-1}(n)}$; their average is the symmetric projection ([[lem-nrqm-composites-hilbert-tensor-construction]], [[lem-nrqm-composites-permutation-sector-projections]], [[lem-nrqm-composites-euclidean-l2-product-unitary]]). The corresponding transverse and symmetric projections in $L_n$, including the polarization-factor permutations, are constructed in step 3.1.
