items/pthm-em-wave-energy-average.md:163:3.3 Clause (e), zero and nonzero amplitudes. If $e=0$ then $b=k\times e/\omega=0$, so $E=B=0$ by [F6] and $u=0$ identically by [F2]; all box integrals and their supremum vanish, so the zero amplitude gives the zero wave and zero total energy. Assume now $e\ne0$ and write $e=a+id$ with $a,d\in\mathbb R^3$; then $u=\varepsilon_0|a\cos\theta-d\sin\theta|^2$ by [F2] and [F6], with $\theta=k\cdot x-\omega t$. Choose a coordinate $j$ with $k_j\ne0$ and put $P:=2\pi/|k_j|$; at fixed $t$ and fixed coordinates $x_i$, $i\ne j$, the map $x_j\mapsto\theta$ is affine with nonzero slope $k_j$, so over any $x_j$-interval of length $P$ the phase runs through an interval of length $2\pi$; the average $\langle u\rangle=\frac{\varepsilon_0}{2}|e|^2$ of step 2.1 is strictly positive here, so $e\ne0$ gives a nonzero wave, and the $2\pi$-periodicity of $\theta\mapsto|a\cos\theta-d\sin\theta|^2$ (by [F9] and the periodicity of the trigonometric functions implicit in the encoding of [F6]) gives $\int_{x_j}^{x_j+P}u\,dx_j=\frac{\varepsilon_0}{|k_j|}\int_0^{2\pi}|a\cos\phi-d\sin\phi|^2d\phi=\frac{\varepsilon_0}{|k_j|}\bigl[\pi|a|^2+\pi|d|^2\bigr]=\frac{\varepsilon_0P}{2}|e|^2>0$, where the elementary integrals of $\cos^2$, $\sin^2$ and $\sin\cos$ over a full period are computed from their derivatives by [F7] and $\int_0^{2\pi}\sin\phi\cos\phi\,d\phi=[\frac12\sin^2\phi]_0^{2\pi}=0$ by [F7]. By [F11] the continuous density is Riemann integrable on every closed box and the iterated integral equals the multiple integral, so for $R=NP$ with a positive integer $N$, integrating first in $x_j$ over the $2N$ full periods of $[-R,R]$ and then over the square $[-R,R]^2$ in the other coordinates gives $\int_{[-R,R]^3}u=(2N)\cdot(2R)^2\cdot\frac{\varepsilon_0P}{2}|e|^2=(2R)^2N\varepsilon_0P|e|^2\to+\infty$ as $N\to\infty$; since $u\ge0$, the Darboux integrals over the boxes are monotone in $R$ by [F11], so the box-exhaustion total energy $\sup_{R>0}\int_{[-R,R]^3}u$ is $+\infty$. [step 1.1, step 2.1, F2, F5, F6, F7, F9, F11, algebra]
