+
+## Statement
+
+Assume countable choice. Let \(r\geq 0\), let \(T=(\mathbb R/\mathbb Z)^r\) with its product Lie-group structure, and let \(M\) be a compact, finite-dimensional, Hausdorff, second-countable smooth manifold without boundary. Let \(a:T\times M\to M\) be a jointly smooth left action. Then \(M\) admits a smooth Riemannian metric invariant under \(a\). Its fixed set
+\[
+F=M^T:=\{p\in M:a(t,p)=p\text{ for every }t\in T\}
+\]
+is a compact embedded smooth submanifold with finitely many connected components, and \(T_pF=(T_pM)^T\) for each \(p\in F\). The orthogonal normal bundle \(\nu_F=(TF)^\perp\subset TM|_F\) has no nonzero \(T\)-fixed vectors in any fiber. On each connected component of \(F\), its normal representation has a finite weight decomposition into nontrivial characters of \(T\), with constant multiplicities; equivalently, \(\nu_F\) admits a smooth \(T\)-invariant complex structure and orientation on each component.
+
+## Statement references
+
+[Fixed set as a submanifold and nontrivial normal weights] [Atiyah–Bott, Section 3, printed pp. 7–9](https://www.math.stonybrook.edu/~mmovshev/MAT570Spring2008/BOOKS/atiyahbott_moment.pdf)
+
+[Fixed component and equivariant normal weights] [Dwivedi et al., Section 9.9, Example 9.48, PDF pp. 85–86](https://www.math.toronto.edu/~jeffrey/mat1312/symplecbookpub.pdf)
+
+## Facts & Assumptions
+
+**Given:** Countable choice, \(T=(\mathbb R/\mathbb Z)^r\), the compact smooth manifold \(M\), and the jointly smooth left action \(a\).
+
+[A1] Countable choice is the principle that every sequence of nonempty sets has a choice function. It supplies the Riemannian metric and geodesic suppliers used below. [[def-countable-choice]]
+
+[F1] A smooth left action is a jointly smooth map satisfying the identity and associativity laws; every fixed group element therefore acts by a diffeomorphism. [[def-smooth-left-action-of-a-lie-group]]
+
+[F2] The product torus is compact and has a normalized translation-invariant probability measure, constructed from Lebesgue measure on a product fundamental cube. [[def-the-one-dimensional-torus-and-normalized-haar-integral]]
+
+[F3] Under countable choice, every smooth manifold admits a smooth positive-definite Riemannian metric. [[thm-every-smooth-manifold-admits-a-riemannian-metric]] The term Riemannian metric means a smooth symmetric covariant two-tensor positive on every nonzero tangent vector. [[def-riemannian-metric-and-riemannian-manifold]]
+
+[F4] A smooth parameter-dependent integrand on a compact rectangle may be differentiated under its Riemann integral; iterating this result gives smooth dependence on finitely many parameters. [[thm-differentiation-under-the-integral-sign-on-a-compact-rectangle]]
+
+[F5] For a Riemannian metric, geodesics exist uniquely locally and depend smoothly on initial tangent data; their evaluation domain is open. [[thm-existence-uniqueness-and-smooth-dependence-of-geodesics]]
+
+[F6] A local Riemannian isometry sends affinely parametrized geodesics to geodesics. [[lem-local-isometries-send-geodesics-to-geodesics]]
+
+[F7] A smooth map with invertible differential at a point is a diffeomorphism on sufficiently small neighborhoods of that point and its image. [[thm-smooth-inverse-function-theorem-on-manifolds]]
+
+## Proof
+
+**Given:** The data in the statement.
+
+1.1 Choose a smooth Riemannian metric \(g_0\) by [F3], let \(\mu\) be the normalized Haar probability measure from [F2], and define
+\[
+g_p(v,w):=\int_T (g_0)_{t^{-1}p}\bigl((d a_{t^{-1}})_p v,(d a_{t^{-1}})_p w\bigr)\,d\mu(t)
+\]
+for \(p\in M\) and \(v,w\in T_pM\). [A1, F2, F3, construct]
+
+1.2 In every pair of local tangent-bundle frames the integrand in [1.1] is smooth in \(p\) and the torus coordinates; repeated application of [F4] on \([0,1]^r\) shows that the coefficients of \(g\) are smooth. Each integrand is a symmetric bilinear form, and for \(v\ne0\) its value on \((v,v)\) is strictly positive because \(d a_{t^{-1}}\) is invertible. Its continuous positive value on compact \(T\) has positive integral, so \(g\) is a Riemannian metric. For \(s\in T\), translation invariance of \(\mu\), with the change of variable \(t=su\), gives \(g_{sp}(d a_s v,d a_s w)=g_p(v,w)\). Thus \(g\) is \(T\)-invariant. [F1, F2, F3, F4, step 1.1, algebra]
+
+2.1 For each \(t\in T\), the equalizer \(\{p:a(t,p)=p\}\) is closed: if \(a(t,p)\ne p\), Hausdorffness gives disjoint neighborhoods of \(p\) and \(a(t,p)\), and continuity gives a neighborhood of \(p\) disjoint from the equalizer. Hence \(F\), their intersection, is closed in compact \(M\), and is compact. [F1, given, step 1.2]
+
+2.2 Fix \(p\in F\). Each \(a_t\) is a \(g\)-isometry fixing \(p\); its differential \(\rho_p(t)=(d a_t)_p\) is an orthogonal linear action on \(T_pM\). The geodesic exponential \(\exp_p\) is defined and smooth near zero by [F5], has differential the identity at zero by the initial-velocity definition of a geodesic, and is a local diffeomorphism there by [F7]. Isometries preserve geodesics by [F6], so uniqueness in [F5] gives \(a_t(\exp_p v)=\exp_p(\rho_p(t)v)\) wherever both sides are defined. Choose a sufficiently small \(\rho_p(T)\)-invariant ball about zero on which \(\exp_p\) is injective; in its image the fixed set is exactly the image of the linear fixed subspace \((T_pM)^T\). These charts show that \(F\) is an embedded submanifold near every \(p\in F\) and \(T_pF=(T_pM)^T\). Since \(F\) is compact, its connected components are finite in number: its components are open in the manifold \(F\), so compactness gives a finite subcover by component neighborhoods. [F1, F5, F6, F7, step 1.2, step 2.1]
+
+3.1 The orthogonal complement \(\nu_{F,p}=(T_pF)^\perp\) has no fixed vector, since the fixed subspace is precisely \(T_pF\) by step 2.2. Complexify \(\nu_{F,p}\); the commuting orthogonal transformations \(\rho_p(t)\) become commuting unitary transformations, so simultaneous diagonalization decomposes the complexified fiber into character eigenspaces. Every character is nontrivial because the fixed eigenspace has been removed. Complex conjugation pairs the \(\chi\) and \(\chi^{-1}\) eigenspaces; choosing one from each pair equips the real normal fiber with a \(T\)-invariant complex structure and orientation. The orthogonal projections to these eigenspaces are the character averages \(\int_T\overline{\chi(t)}\rho_p(t)\,d\mu(t)\); their coefficients vary smoothly with \(p\) by [F4]. Their ranks are integer-valued and continuous, hence locally constant, so they form smooth subbundles with constant multiplicities on connected components. Compactness of \(F\) and finite fiber dimension make the union of weights finite. [F2, F4, step 1.2, step 2.2]
+
+4.1 If \(r=0\), the action is trivial, \(F=M\), and \(\nu_F=0\), so the weight assertion is vacuous. If \(M\) is empty, all asserted submanifolds and bundles are empty. Otherwise the construction applies componentwise and uses no assumption on connectedness of \(M\). [A1, F1, F2, F3, F5, step 2.1, step 3.1] ∎

