+---
+
+## Statement
+
+Assume countable choice. Let \(T=(\mathbb R/\mathbb Z)^r\) with \(r\geq1\), and let \(H\subsetneq T\) be a closed subgroup. A character means a smooth group homomorphism from \(T\) to the multiplicative unit circle \(S^1=\{z\in\mathbb C:|z|=1\}\). Then there is a nontrivial character \(\chi:T\to S^1\) whose restriction to all of \(H\) is the trivial character.
+
+## Statement references
+
+[Proper isotropy character in the localization argument] [Atiyah–Bott, Section 3, printed pp. 6–8](https://www.math.stonybrook.edu/~mmovshev/MAT570Spring2008/BOOKS/atiyahbott_moment.pdf)
+
+[Closed-subgroup quotient and exponential construction] [Etingof, Theorem 4.1 and quotient charts, printed p. 28](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18.745-lie-groups-and-lie-algebras-i-fall-2020/mit18_745_f20_lec_full.pdf)
+
+## Facts & Assumptions
+
+**Given:** Countable choice, the product torus \(T\), and a proper closed subgroup \(H\).
+
+[A1] Countable choice supplies the embedded Lie subgroup and smooth quotient structures used here. [[def-countable-choice]]
+
+[F1] For a closed subgroup \(H\) of a finite-dimensional Lie group \(T\), the quotient \(Q=T/H\) is a smooth Lie group, the quotient map is a surjective submersion, and its dimension is \(\dim T-\dim H\). [[thm-quotient-manifold-by-a-closed-lie-subgroup]]
+
+[F2] The exponential map of a finite-dimensional Lie group is smooth, has identity differential at zero, and is obtained from its one-parameter subgroups. [[thm-the-lie-group-exponential-map-is-smooth-with-identity-differential-at-zero]] [[thm-one-parameter-subgroups-are-integral-curves-of-left-invariant-fields]] [[prop-exponential-scales-one-parameter-subgroups]] [[def-one-parameter-subgroup-of-a-lie-group]]
+
+[F3] A smooth map with invertible differential is a diffeomorphism on some open neighborhoods. [[thm-smooth-inverse-function-theorem-on-manifolds]]
+
+[F4] A continuous image of a compact space is compact. [[thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps]]
+
+## Proof
+
+**Given:** The data in the statement.
+
+1.1 Since \(T\) is compact and connected, \(Q=T/H\) is compact and connected. By [F1], it is a Lie group. It is abelian because \(T\) is abelian. It is not a point because \(H\subsetneq T\); a connected zero-dimensional Lie group is discrete and hence a point, so \(Q\) has dimension \(d\geq1\). [F1, F4, given, algebra]
+
+1.2 Let \(\mathfrak q=T_eQ\). The exponential map \(\exp_Q:\mathfrak q\to Q\) has differential the identity at zero by [F2], so [F3] makes it a diffeomorphism from a neighborhood of zero onto a neighborhood of the identity. Since \(Q\) is abelian, \(\exp_Q(X+Y)=\exp_Q(X)\exp_Q(Y)\): the product of the one-parameter subgroups with velocities \(X,Y\) is a one-parameter subgroup with velocity \(X+Y\), and uniqueness of the integral curve gives the identity. Thus \(\exp_Q(\mathfrak q)\) is a subgroup containing an open identity neighborhood, hence is open. Its other cosets are open, so its complement is open; connectedness of \(Q\) gives \(\exp_Q(\mathfrak q)=Q\). The kernel \(\Lambda=\ker(\exp_Q)\) is discrete because the exponential is injective near zero. [F2, F3, step 1.1]
+
+2.1 The linear span of \(\Lambda\) is all of \(\mathfrak q\). Otherwise the quotient linear map \(\mathfrak q\to\mathfrak q/\operatorname{span}_{\mathbb R}\Lambda\) factors through the compact space \(Q=\mathfrak q/\Lambda\) and is onto a nonzero real vector space; that is impossible by [F4]. A discrete additive subgroup of a finite-dimensional real vector space that spans it is a lattice: choose a shortest nonzero vector \(\lambda_1\); its intersection with the line \(\mathbb R\lambda_1\) is \(\mathbb Z\lambda_1\). Orthogonal projection of \(\Lambda\) to \(\lambda_1^\perp\) is discrete, because any projected sequence tending to zero can be adjusted by integer multiples of \(\lambda_1\) into a bounded interval along that line, after which discreteness makes it eventually constant. Induction on \(d\) gives a \(\mathbb Z\)-basis \(\lambda_1,\ldots,\lambda_d\) of \(\Lambda\) that is also a real basis of \(\mathfrak q\). [F4, step 1.2, algebra]
+
+2.2 Define the nonzero real linear functional \(\ell:\mathfrak q\to\mathbb R\) by \(\ell(\lambda_1)=1\) and \(\ell(\lambda_j)=0\) for \(2\leq j\leq d\). Then \(\ell(\Lambda)\subseteq\mathbb Z\). Define \(\chi_Q(\exp_Q X):=\exp(2\pi i\,\ell(X))\). This is well defined because two logarithms differ by an element of \(\Lambda\), and it is a smooth homomorphism because \(\exp_Q\) is a surjective local diffeomorphism and additive on this abelian Lie algebra. It is nontrivial: \(\exp_Q(\lambda_1/2)\ne e\), w

