+
+## Plan and inventory comparison
+
+The complete design section at `research/plan-advanced-qft-and-supersymmetry-track.md` §“Borel Localization for Compact Torus Actions” and the corresponding rows in `research/plan-spec.json` agree on both page IDs, order 964, title, companion, `quantum-field-theory-mathematics` category, mathematics library, and the A-page prerequisite `qftx-equivariant-cohomology`. The proposed inventory names only `thm-borel-localization-for-compact-torus-actions` as its future A-page theorem and explicitly separates noncompact and compact-support variants. The empty run-manifest item arrays are authoring placeholders. No plan/design conflict was found; the A page keeps the compact smooth Borel theorem, and the B page carries finite-dimensional examples.
+
+The owner direction dated 2026-10-23 controls the scope. It requires complete local mathematics, actual inspected supplier interfaces, substantial B-page examples, no claim that finite-dimensional Borel localization proves continuum field-theory localization, and frontmatter-only A/B pages.
+
+## Supplier interface and readiness
+
+The required supplier page is `qftx-equivariant-cohomology`. Its current A-page frontmatter and the actual carriers `def-torus-equivariant-cohomology-cartan-model` and `lem-torus-classifying-space-cohomology` were read, including their complete local proofs. The definition sets
+$H_T^*(M;\mathbb R)=H^*(ET\times_T M;\mathbb R)$ for the product Milnor model and a smooth left action, identifies $H_T^*(\ast;\mathbb R)=\mathbb R[u_1,\ldots,u_r]$ with degree-two generators, and separately defines the Cartan complex with $d_T^2=0$ on invariants. It explicitly makes no general Borel–Cartan comparison. The coefficient lemma uses $T=(\mathbb R/\mathbb Z)^r$, the right Milnor product model, tautological-line Euler classes and the displayed period-one Lie algebra basis; its proof is conditional on full AC.
+
+Before any escalation, `loadStep1Record` and `step1Decision` were run for both in-run supplier items. Both return `Readiness record missing`. The carrier statements and proofs are present and match the planned Borel interface, but the absent current Step 1 records remain an unresolved readiness prerequisite. This is not inferred from their `draft` statuses or lack of independent approval. Batch 8 will use the exact inspected definition conditionally and will not mark consumers ready until the supplier readiness is current.
+
+## Sources read for this A page
+
+- M. F. Atiyah and R. Bott, “The Moment Map and Equivariant Cohomology,” *Topology* 23 (1984), §2 on equivariant pushforward/Thom classes and §3, Lemma 3.3, Proposition 3.4, Theorem 3.5 and equation (3.7), printed pp. 4–9. Retrieved and read from `https://www.math.stonybrook.edu/~mmovshev/MAT570Spring2008/BOOKS/atiyahbott_moment.pdf`. It supports the fixed-point restriction theorem, orbit-isotropy torsion mechanism, normal-weight Euler class and Gysin route. Its finite-dimensional hypotheses are retained.
+- Shubham Dwivedi, Jonathan Herman, Lisa C. Jeffrey and Theo van den Hurk, *Hamiltonian Group Actions and Equivariant Cohomology*, SpringerBriefs in Mathematics (2019), §9.9, Example 9.48, and §9.10, Theorem 9.50 and the displayed proof routes, PDF pp. 85–87. Retrieved and read from `https://www.math.toronto.edu/~jeffrey/mat1312/symplecbookpub.pdf`. This independent book treatment supports the fixed-set submanifold, normal-weight and equivariant Euler-inverse computations. Its short reference to an injectivity step is not used as a substitute for the local proof in batch 8.
+- Allen Hatcher, *Algebraic Topology*, §3.2, Theorem 3.19, printed pp. 220–222. Retrieved and read from `https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/ATch3.pdf`. It supplies the integral projective-space ring and the standard finite-stage context for the separately proved rational coefficient calculation. The local argument adds the coefficient-change and finite-product steps.
+
+All three source locators, actual read extents, limitations, and per-item dispositions are recorded in `research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-8.coverage.json`. `source-fetch-check --stamp` remains a required post-authoring verification.
+
+## Completed item checkpoint
+
+### `lem-qftx-compact-torus-fixed-locus-normal-weights`
+
+**Claim and conventions.** Under $AC_\omega$, for compact $T=(\mathbb R/\mathbb Z)^r$ acting smoothly on a compact finite-dimensional boundaryless manifold $M$, averaging a Riemannian metric gives a $T$-invariant metric; $F=M^T$ is a compact embedded submanifold with $T_pF=(T_pM)^T$; the normal fibers have no trivial representation and split smoothly into finitely many nonzero weight bundles on each component. The proof fixes the product-torus Haar measure and the geodesic-exponential convention; it does not assume Hamiltonian or symplectic data.
+
+**Dependencies.** `def-countable-choice`, `def-smooth-left-action-of-a-lie-group`, `def-the-one-dimensional-torus-and-normalized-haar-integral`, `def-riemannian-metric-and-riemannian-manifold`, `thm-every-smooth-manifold-admits-a-riemannian-metric`, `thm-differentiation-under-the-integral-sign-on-a-compact-rectangle`, `thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps`, `thm-existence-uniqueness-and-smooth-dependence-of-geodesics`, `lem-local-isometries-send-geodesics-to-geodesics`, and `thm-smooth-inverse-function-theorem-on-manifolds`. All are imported mathematical suppliers; the item level is 0.
+
+**Decisions and checks.** Statement and proof are authored with no inline links in the statement; all input uses are in Facts and proof. Scoped render, dependency, proof-layout, contract and source-fetch checks remain outstanding. No unresolved mathematical uncertainty has been identified for this item. Next: finish the equivariant slice/tubular lemma, then the proper-isotropy character and coefficient-ring prerequisites.
+
+### lem-qftx-compact-torus-equivariant-slice-neighborhoods
+
+**Claim and conventions.** For any compact invariant embedded submanifold of a compact torus manifold with invariant metric, the normal exponential gives an equivariant tubular disk. Applied to an orbit \(T/H\), its normal bundle is \(T\times_HV\); because \(T\) is abelian, all stabilizers in the slice are contained in the proper isotropy subgroup \(H\). The uniform disk and injectivity arguments use compactness of the zero section and countable choice only for the displayed sequence of hypothetical collisions.
+
+**Dependencies.** lem-qftx-compact-torus-fixed-locus-normal-weights, thm-every-orbit-is-an-injectively-immersed-homogeneous-space, thm-existence-uniqueness-and-smooth-dependence-of-geodesics, lem-local-isometries-send-geodesics-to-geodesics, thm-smooth-inverse-function-theorem-on-manifolds, thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps, and def-countable-choice. Its in-batch level is 1.
+
+**Decisions and checks.** The orbit is embedded because \(T/H\) is compact and \(M\) Hausdorff; the normal exponential's differential is \(u+w\) at the zero section, and the collision-sequence argument supplies uniform injectivity. No unrelated supplier is edited. Scoped checks remain outstanding. Next: prove the proper-isotropy character and rational coefficient interfaces.
+
+### lem-qftx-proper-closed-subgroup-has-annihilating-character
+
+**Claim and conventions.** For every proper closed subgroup \(H\) of a product torus \(T\), the quotient \(Q=T/H\) is a positive-dimensional compact connected abelian Lie group. Its exponential kernel is a full lattice; a dual lattice functional constructs a nontrivial smooth character of \(Q\), whose pullback to \(T\) is trivial on all of \(H\). This is the annihilator required by the finite orbit-slice proof.
+
+**Dependencies.** def-countable-choice, thm-quotient-manifold-by-a-closed-lie-subgroup, thm-the-lie-group-exponential-map-is-smooth-with-identity-differential-at-zero, thm-one-parameter-subgroups-are-integral-curves-of-left-invariant-fields, prop-exponential-scales-one-parameter-subgroups, def-one-parameter-subgroup-of-a-lie-group, thm-smooth-inverse-function-theorem-on-manifolds, and thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps. All are imported mathematical suppliers; dependency level is 0.
+
+**Decisions and checks.** The proof constructs a character trivial on the full subgroup, which implies the planned weaker identity-component statement. It uses ACω only through the closed-subgroup quotient and exponential interfaces. Scoped checks remain outstanding. Next: prove the rational \(BT\) coefficient/character-class formula, then define the character localization using it and the inspected real supplier.
