+    - title: Allen Hatcher, Algebraic Topology
+      url: https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/ATch3.pdf
+      locator: Section 3.2, Theorem 3.19, printed pp. 220–222.
+landmark: false
+---
+
+## Statement
+
+Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let \(r\geq0\), let \(T=(\mathbb R/\mathbb Z)^r\), and use \(E_T=(E_{S^1})^r\), the product of the right Milnor join models, with \(BT=E_T/T\). For the \(i\)th coordinate character \(\chi_i([t_1,\ldots,t_r])=\exp(2\pi i t_i)\), let \(L_i=E_T\times_T\mathbb C_{\chi_i}\) be the associated complex line bundle over \(BT\), with the right diagonal equivalence \((e,z)\cdot t=(e\cdot t,\chi_i(t)^{-1}z)\), and put \(u_i=c_1(L_i)\in H^2(BT;\mathbb Q)\). Then
+\[
+BT\cong(\mathbb{CP}^{\infty})^r,\qquad H^*(BT;\mathbb Q)\cong\mathbb Q[u_1,\ldots,u_r],\qquad |u_i|=2.
+\]
+Every smooth character \(\chi:T\to S^1\) is uniquely \(\chi_{\mathbf n}([t])=\exp(2\pi i\sum_i n_it_i)\) for \(\mathbf n=(n_1,\ldots,n_r)\in\mathbb Z^r\), and its associated line satisfies \(c_1(L_{\chi_{\mathbf n}})=\sum_i n_i u_i\). In particular a nontrivial character has a nonzero degree-two class. For each \(k\in\{\mathbb Q,\mathbb R\}\), \(H_q(BT;k)\) is finite-dimensional in every degree \(q\). For \(r=0\), \(BT\) is a point and its rational cohomology is \(\mathbb Q\).
+
+## Statement references
+
+[Integral projective-space cohomology ring underlying the rational calculation] [Hatcher, Section 3.2, Theorem 3.19, printed pp. 220–222](https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/ATch3.pdf)
+
+## Facts & Assumptions
+
+**Given:** AC, \(r\geq0\), the product torus, and the product Milnor model in the statement.
+
+[A1] Assume the Axiom of Choice. [[def-axiom-of-choice]]
+
+[F1] The multiplicative unit circle is a compact metrizable topological group; with basepoint \(1\), it has a finite CW structure with \(1\) as a zero-cell. [[lem-unit-circle-is-a-compact-metrizable-topological-group]]
+
+[F2] A relative CW subcomplex inclusion is a cofibration, and a based space is well-pointed when the basepoint inclusion is a cofibration. [[prop-relative-cw-inclusions-are-cofibrations]] [[def-compactly-generated-based-space-and-well-pointed-object]]
+
+[F3] For a well-pointed topological group of CW type, the Milnor join is free and contractible; for \(S^1\) its quotient is the weak CW colimit \(\mathbb{CP}^{\infty}\). [[def-milnor-infinite-join-model-of-eg]] [[thm-milnor-join-model-is-a-contractible-free-g-space]]
+
+[F4] With \(u=e(\gamma_{\mathbb R})\) for the complex-oriented tautological line, \(H^*(\mathbb{CP}^{\infty};\mathbb Z)=\mathbb Z[u]\), and integral homology is \(\mathbb Z\) in even degrees and zero in odd degrees. [[lem-cohomology-ring-of-infinite-complex-projective-space]]
+
+[F5] For any space and coefficient group \(G\), the natural sequence has kernel \(\operatorname{Ext}^1(H_{n-1}(-;\mathbb Z),G)\) and quotient \(\operatorname{Hom}(H_n(-;\mathbb Z),G)\). [[thm-topological-universal-coefficient-short-exact-sequence-for-cohomology]]
+
+[F6] For a free chain complex over a PID, homology after tensoring has the natural exact sequence with \(H_n(C)\otimes G\) and \(\operatorname{Tor}_1(H_{n-1}(C),G)\). [[thm-universal-coefficient-theorem-for-homology-over-a-pid]]
+
+[F7] Over a PID under AC, the cohomological cross product is a graded-ring isomorphism when one factor has finite-free homology in every degree. [[thm-cohomological-kunneth-cross-product-is-a-ring-isomorphism]]
+
+[F8] First Chern classes are additive under tensor product and change sign under dualization; for a complex line, \(c_1\) is the Euler class of its complex-oriented underlying real plane. [[prop-first-chern-class-of-tensor-dual-and-conjugate-lines]] [[def-chern-classes-from-the-projective-bundle-relation]]
+
+[F9] The complex exponential has derivative itself, and its period-one values \(\exp(2\pi i a)=1\) occur exactly for \(a\in\mathbb Z\). [[thm-complex-exponential-is-entire-with-derivative-itself]] [[thm-sine-cosine-zero-sets-and-fundamental-period]]
+
+[F10] A differentiable real function with zero derivative on an interval is constant. [[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]]
+
+[F11] Over a field and under AC, singular homology of a product is the direct-sum tensor product of the factor homologies in each total degree. [[cor-field-kunneth-isomorphism-for-homology-of-products]]
+
+## Proof
+
+**Given:** AC, \(r\geq0\), the stated product torus, and the product Milnor model.
+
+1.1 The identity point is a zero-cell of the finite CW circle, so its inclusion is a relative CW subcomplex inclusion and is a cofibration by [F2]. Together with compact metrizability from [F1], this makes \(S^1\) a well-pointed group of CW type in the sense of [F2]. Hence [F3] applies. [F1, F2]
+
+1.2 The product \(E_T=(E_{S^1})^r\) is free for the coordinatewise right \(T\)-action, since a point fixed by \(t\) has every coordinate fixed and each factor action is free. It is contractible because it is a finite product of contractible spaces. Each Milnor quotient map is open: the saturation of an open set is the union of its translates. The finite product quotient map is therefore open and its fibers are exactly the \(T\)-orbits, so it induces \(BT\cong (BS^1)^r\cong(\mathbb{CP}^{\infty})^r\) by [F3]. For \(r=0\) this is the one-point product. [F3, step 1.1, construct]
+
+2.1 On one \(\mathbb{CP}^{\infty}\) factor, apply [F5] with \(G=\mathbb Q\). By [F4], each integral homology group is free, so the Ext term is zero and evaluation identifies \(H^n(\mathbb{CP}^{\infty};\mathbb Q)\) with \(\operatorname{Hom}(H_n(\mathbb{CP}^{\infty};\mathbb Z),\mathbb Q)\). The natural coefficient map sends each integral generator \(u^k\) to the nonzero generator of the corresponding rational group. Thus the rational ring is \(\mathbb Q[u_{\mathbb Q}]\), with \(|u_{\mathbb Q}|=2\). [A1, F4, F5]
+
+2.2 Apply [F6] to the integral singular chain complex with \(G=\mathbb Q\). Each integral homology group in [F4] is free, so the Tor term vanishes and \(H_n(\mathbb{CP}^{\infty};\mathbb Q)\) is \(\mathbb Q\) for even \(n\) and zero for odd \(n\); it is finite free in every degree. [A1, F4, F6, given]
+
+2.3 For \(k=\mathbb Q\) or \(\mathbb R\), [F6] applied to the same integral chain complex and \(G=k\) gives \(H_n(\mathbb{CP}^{\infty};k)=k\) for even \(n\) and zero for odd \(n\), since the integral groups in [F4] are free. Iterating [F11] over the finite product in step 1.2 identifies \(H_n(BT;k)\) with a direct sum over the finitely many \(r\)-tuples of nonnegative degrees summing to \(n\). Every summand is either zero or one-dimensional over \(k\), so \(H_n(BT;k)\) is finite-dimensional, and therefore finite-free, for every \(n\). For \(r=0\), only the degree-zero group \(k\) occurs. [A1, F4, F6, F11, step 1.2, algebra]
+
+3.1 Iterating the ring cross product [F7] over the finite product \((\mathbb{CP}^{\infty})^r\) identifies its rational cohomology ring with the graded tensor product of \(r\) copies of \(\mathbb Q[u_{\mathbb Q}]\), using the finite-free homology in step 2.2. All generators have even degree, so the graded tensor multiplication has no sign changes and is \(\mathbb Q[u_1,\ldots,u_r]\). [A1, F7, step 2.1, step 2.2]
+
+4.1 Let \(\chi:T\to S^1\) be a smooth character and restrict it to each coordinate one-parameter subgroup \(\gamma_i(s)=[0,\ldots,s,\ldots,0]\). Write \(f_i(s)=\chi(\gamma_i(s))\). The homomorphism law gives \(f_i(s+h)=f_i(s)f_i(h)\); differentiating in \(h\) at zero yields \(f_i'(s)=f_i(s)f_i'(0)\). Since \(|f_i(s)|=1\), differentiation of \(|f_i(s)|^2=1\) at zero shows \(f_i'(0)=2\pi i a_i\) for a real \(a_i\). By [F9], the derivative of \(e^{-2\pi i a_i s}f_i(s)\) is zero, so [F10] makes it constant \(1\); hence \(f_i(s)=e^{2\pi i a_i s}\). Since \(\gamma_i(1)\) is the identity, \(f_i(1)=1\) and [F9] gives \(a_i=n_i\in\mathbb Z\). The product decomposition of \(T\) now gives \(\chi([t])=e^{2\pi i\sum_i n_i t_i}\). Uniqueness follows by restricting again to each \(\gamma_i\). [F9, F10, given, construct]
+
+5.1 For the coordinate character \(\chi_i\), the associated line \(L_i\) restricts on each finite Milnor quotient stage to the tautological complex line: the map \([e,z]\mapsto z e\) is well defined under the right diagonal equivalence and identifies the fiber with the line spanned by \(e\). By [F4] and [F8], \(u_i=c_1(L_i)\) is a rational degree-two generator. For \(\mathbf n\in\mathbb Z^r\), the associated line is the tensor product of \(L_i^{\otimes n_i}\), interpreting negative powers as dual lines; additivity and duality in [F8] give \(c_1(L_{\chi_{\mathbf n}})=\sum_i n_i u_i\). This is nonzero exactly when \(\mathbf n\ne0\), by the polynomial basis in step 3.1. [F3, F4, F8, step 1.2, step 3.1, step 4.1]
+
+6.1 For \(r=0\), \(T\) and \(BT\) are points, the cohomology is \(\mathbb Q\) in degree zero, and there are no nontrivial characters. The finite product, universal-coefficient, and ring arguments above add no additional restriction beyond AC. [A1, F3, F4, F5, F6, F7, step 2.1, step 3.1] ∎
